## 2026-08-27  richness floors/caps, and two decisions the 3b probe reversed

The sampler's `tissue_ratio` gate is gone. It scored the same quantity as the
richness buckets -- background fraction -- and the two disagreeing produced the
475/500 corpus of 2026-08-26, which was read at the time as a property of the
slides. A cap of zero on the top buckets says the same thing once.

Seven buckets, each with a FLOOR and a CAP (two constraints of different
natures: a cap is always achievable, a floor demands the slide has the tiles):

    bucket     background   floor   cap    target
    bg00_15      < 15 %       5 %   15 %    15 %      the unassigned 30 % is
    bg15_30     15 - 30 %    15 %   25 %    25 %      split evenly over the
    bg30_50     30 - 50 %    50 %   60 %    60 %      three buckets that ASKED
    bg50_70     50 - 70 %      -    20 %     0        -- a cap forbids, it does
    bg70_85     70 - 85 %      -    20 %     0        not request. So 50_70 and
    bg85_95     85 - 95 %      -     0       0        70_85 receive nothing
    bg95_100     > 95 %        -     0       0        unless something falls
                                                      short: they are spillway.

A zero cap binds the INHERITANCE set too, and truncates the chain rather than
letting it hole. Everything else is advisory for a carried centre; a gate the
carried centres walk through is not a gate.

### What the 12-slide probe measured (tile 256, each cell asked 500)

    ds   admissible   taken |  bg00_15    bg15_30    bg30_50   below  spill
    1       461,141    6,000 |  ->900      ->1,500    ->3,600       0      0
    2       123,624    6,000 |  ->900      ->1,500    ->3,600       0      0
    4        32,474    6,000 |  ->900      ->1,500    ->3,366      84    234
    8         8,465    5,104 |  ->798      ->1,166    ->1,618   1,505  1,522
    16        2,242    2,064 |  ->345      ->  312    ->  524   3,150    883
    32          583      578 |  -> 76      ->   71    ->  148   3,922    283

The contract holds exactly where supply allows. From ds 16 up, `taken` is
essentially `admissible`: the binding constraint is no longer the quotas but
how many DISJOINT positions a rung holds on a finite slide.

### Reversed: align-min is out

spec.md 6.5's rule was "align-min if the worst rung still has hundreds,
loss-weight if it has forty". The 2026-08-26 answer was 1784 of 2000 -- but
that was the tissue gate exhausting a rejection budget, not a count of
positions. The real worst cell is 18 (S1104233 at ds 32). align-min truncates
every rung to 18: 1,296 tiles against 8,465 available at ds 8 alone.

So `--balance none` for v1, unbalanced and recorded as such. Not loss-weight
either: the rung weights are a second decision and the corpus has to exist
first. The lever is more slides -- ds 32 needs about 25 to reach the 1,200 that
12 reach at ds 16.

### Left as a switch: RichnessConfig.floor_frame

At ds 32 the floors are a share of `n_per_rung` while `n_taken` is a twentieth
of it, so they have no object: the realised mix is 13/12/26/33/16 against a
target of 15/25/60/0/0 -- nearly half the rung above 50 per cent background.

    'ask'    (default, chosen for v1) floors are a share of what was asked.
             Keeps the tiles, the mix drifts.
    'taken'  floors are a share of what is ACHIEVABLE; the rung scales down to
             the largest count whose mix the supply can hold. Same ds 32
             becomes ~250 tiles at 15/25/60.

Both frames agree exactly wherever supply exceeds the ask, which is ds 1-4, so
the default costs nothing there. `probe_tile_yield` runs BOTH arms every cell
and records `n_got_if_taken`, so the number that would decide the switch is in
front of whoever flips it.
